  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\n- Gusty conditions this afternoon with winds 25-35 mph.\n\nWarm air advection driven by southerly flow has led to quite the \nwarm day across the Michiana region and northwestern Ohio this \nafternoon with highs already in the low to mid 70s. Mostly clear \nskies combined with ample mixing may allow highs today to challenge \nthe daily record of 76F. Regardless, it will feel more like late \nsummer than November across the area. Increased mixing depth around \npeak heating this afternoon will also tap into an approaching LLJ \nwith gusts as high as 35 mph, highest across Northwest Indiana into \nSouthwest Michigan.\n\n- Breezy conditions and rain showers expected Tuesday Night into \nWednesday\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a robust trough across the middle \nMississippi Valley at present forecast to move into the region \ntonight into Wednesday. The surface reflection of this will be in \nthe form of a cold front crossing the area during the day, with \nwidespread showers out ahead of it. Expect rain much of the day \nWednesday given the slow moving nature of the front as the surface \nlow becomes vertically aligned with the parent trough. A dry slot \nmoving in later Wednesday ends the rain showers, with northwest Ohio \nlast to dry out in the evening. REFS probabilistic guidance favors \nless than 1/4\" for most, with rainfall as high as 0.5\" across our \nsoutheast Indiana counties into NW Ohio.\n\n- Cooler with lake driven rain showers into the weekend\n\nMore robust cold air arrives with persistent troughing Friday into \nthe weekend behind a cold frontal boundary. 850 mb temps fall to \nnear zero, with lake temps still in the mid teens. This will be \nsufficient instability wise to generate lake effect rain showers \nThursday Night into Friday. The trough and coldest mid-level air \nwill depart to the east Saturday with northwest flow into early next \nweek. How much if any precipitation we receive if any will depend on \nshortwaves embedded within the northwest flow as well as whether a \nridge across the central CONUS drives flow be more zonal for a \nperiod early next week.",
